Subject: Re: Pr e-final partial release of the P?S/8 PAL Technical Guide.
To: P?S/8 <Unknown>


Just yet another quick update.  One of the typo hunters was temporarily 
unavailable and is actively working on yet another generation of edit, I 
didn't even mention here.  As such, it is modestly even more incompatible 
with the posted manual fragment.

Hopefully shortly, an actual tentatively final actual complete release.

I want to get this manual "right" because there is so much info in it, some 
of which goes beyond merely P?S/8 usage, and goes into other assemblers as 
well, and some concepts and examples not well known, quite a few of which 
apply to the likes of OS/8 PAL8.  [Where relevant, specific comparisons are 
drawn].

Please be a bit more patient, I apologize for the cumulative delays, but it 
will be worth it.

cjl

ps: I an no longer actively working on it and have moved onto other works.  
Of course if they find anything, i will quickly finish it; at this stage, I 
cannot foresee of anything other than incidental.


On Thursday, February 15, 2018 at 11:16:37 AM UTC-5, cjl wrote:
>
> Just a brief update:
>
> 1) Some of the specific content of the manual fragment have already been 
> modestly changed; most changes fix some "invisible" tab problems in the 
> word-processing.; in a few places some words have been changed to clarify 
> what is stated there; not worth posting another fragment as the entire 
> proof-reading and editing is under way and hopefully will be completed 
> within a few days..
>
> 2) As suggested/urged/insisted, I have added a Glossary of Terms section 
> at the end to make this document more "stand-alone".to an arms-length 
> reader.  One of the problems is when you write the code, documenting that 
> code can be more troublesome than documenting the work of others.
>
> cjl [I'm not a real tech writer, but I play one on the Internet]
>
> On Monday, February 12, 2018 at 3:44:12 AM UTC-5, cjl wrote:
>>
>> Due to the unexpected proof-reading delay [being done by others; in 
>> general authors do not find their own mistakes] I am releasing here and now 
>> the first about 20 percent of the manual as it stands.  Be advised:
>>
>> 1) It is slightly obsolete [but hopefully only typos.
>>
>> Of course if you find any, please post corrections here on the group, or 
>> e-mail me personally at CLASy...@gmail.com.
>>
>> Note: In reading this just now [having been away from it for over a 
>> month] I myself found 4 minor formatting issues in the realm of minor 
>> typos.  This does not change the content per se, but there is at least one 
>> occurrence of arguably inappropriate use of italics and at least three 
>> tabulation errors [caused by the consequences of word-processing]
>>
>> I guess I should amend the above to see that authors do not generally 
>> find their own mistakes as long as the work is fresh in the mind of the 
>> author.  As such I eagerly await their findings.  The goal is a final 
>> edition that is complete and accurate.  [Note: I am not a professional tech 
>> writer; I only play one on the Internet.]
>>
>> 2) It takes be a bit of work to create a complete paste-up of any 
>> manual.  This is NOT merely the single output of any one word-processor.  
>> For most people, these nit-pick differences are totally unimportant.  That 
>> said, if you are properly anal, consider being a voluntary typo-finder; I 
>> will appreciate it.
>>
>> I wanted a wider audience to get a handle on the nature of the quality 
>> [or lack thereof] to be found in my manual writing; I will accept all blame 
>> and praises equally.  As such, comments and reviews are welcome and should 
>> be posted either here or as e-mail to me personally as you see fit.
>>
>> 3) There is a lot more "stuff" in the rest of the complete version; your 
>> patience is noted.
>>
>> 4) I am currently writing some code and also working on a different 
>> manual; there are many.  The more I do, the less I have to do.
>>
>> 5) A complete manual set for P?S/8 will be several times larger than all 
>> known documentation of OS/8.  This is appropriate.
>>
>> cjl
>>
>
